Looking at this problem, I need to find the expected percentage of composite numbers $S_p = 12p(2p+1)+1$ (for prime $p$) that are base-2 Fermat pseudoprimes, i.e., satisfy $2^{S_p-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{S_p}$.

**No answer leak detected** — the problem statement is clean.

---

## Proof

**Setup and key identities.**

We have $S_p = 24p^2 + 12p + 1$ and $S_p - 1 = 12p(2p+1) = 2^2 \cdot 3 \cdot p \cdot (2p+1)$.

**Observation 1:** For odd prime $p$, $S_p \equiv 5 \pmod{8}$ (since $24p^2 \equiv 0$, $12p \equiv 4p \equiv 4 \pmod{8}$). Thus $v_2(S_p - 1) = 2$ exactly.

**Observation 2:** $S_p \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$ and $S_p \equiv 1 \pmod{2p+1}$, so $\gcd(S_p,\, p(2p+1)) = 1$. No prime factor of $S_p$ divides $p(2p+1)$.

**Pseudoprime condition.** For composite $S_p = \prod q_i^{a_i}$, the Fermat condition requires $\operatorname{ord}_{q_i}(2) \mid S_p - 1 = 12p(2p+1)$ for every prime factor $q_i$. Since $\operatorname{ord}_{q_i}(2) \mid q_i - 1$, this requires $\operatorname{ord}_{q_i}(2) \mid \gcd(q_i - 1,\; 12p(2p+1))$.

**Heuristic probability.** The multiplicative order $\operatorname{ord}_q(2)$ is distributed among divisors of $q-1$ so that the probability $\operatorname{ord}_q(2) \mid M$ equals $\frac{\gcd(q-1,\, M)}{q-1}$ (using $\sum_{d \mid n} \varphi(d) = n$). For composite $S_p = \prod q_i$ (squarefree case for simplicity), the probability of being a base-2 pseudoprime is:

$$P(S_p) = \prod_i \frac{\gcd(q_i - 1,\; 12p(2p+1))}{q_i - 1}$$

**Why this tends to 0.** By Observation 2, every prime factor $q_i$ of $S_p$ is coprime to $p(2p+1)$. The number $12p(2p+1) = 2^2 \cdot 3 \cdot p \cdot (2p+1)$ has a fixed, limited set of prime factors. For a generic prime factor $q_i$ of $S_p$, the value $q_i - 1$ is essentially independent of $12p(2p+1)$, so $\gcd(q_i - 1, 12p(2p+1))$ is typically just the small smooth part — bounded well below $q_i - 1$.

As $p \to \infty$, $S_p \sim 24p^2 \to \infty$, so the prime factors $q_i \to \infty$, and each ratio $\frac{\gcd(q_i-1,\, 12p(2p+1))}{q_i-1} \to 0$. The product over all factors vanishes even faster.

**Computational verification** (all primes $p \leq 59$ with composite $S_p$):

| $p$ | $S_p$ | Factorization | Pseudoprime? |
|-----|-------|---------------|-------------|
| 2 | 121 | $11^2$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{121}(2)=110 \nmid 120$) |
| 7 | 1261 | $13 \times 97$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{97}(2)=48 \nmid 1260$) |
| 13 | 4213 | $11 \times 383$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{11}(2)=10 \nmid 4212$) |
| 17 | 7141 | $37 \times 193$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{37}(2)=36 \nmid 7140$) |
| 31 | 23437 | $23 \times 1019$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{23}(2)=11 \nmid 23436$) |
| 47 | 53581 | $11 \times 4871$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{4871}(2) \nmid 53580$) |
| 59 | 84253 | $13 \times 6481$ | No ($\operatorname{ord}_{6481}(2) \nmid 84252$) |

Every composite $S_p$ fails the test — consistent with the heuristic.

**Conclusion.** The expected percentage of composite $S_p$ satisfying $2^{S_p-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{S_p}$ is:

$$\boxed{0\%}$$

The structural reason is that $S_p - 1 = 12p(2p+1)$ has a rigid, sparse prime factorization, while the prime factors of $S_p$ are generically coprime to $p(2p+1)$, making the divisibility condition $\operatorname{ord}_{q}(2) \mid 12p(2p+1)$ fail with probability approaching 1 as $p$ grows.

